<%# The objections, answered where a skeptic will actually look for them.
    Every answer that could be a hedge is a straight yes or no first, then
    the qualification — a FAQ that dodges reads worse than no FAQ. %>

<div class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-8">
  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Does it phone home?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      No. There is no telemetry, no analytics, no update check, no licence
      server, and no address of ours anywhere in it. The app reaches the
      network only where you have connected something yourself — a mailbox,
      a feed, a calendar, a hosted model if you chose one — and those go to
      that provider, not to us.
      <%= link_to "The full list →", about_page_path("privacy"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Do I need to be technical?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      To install it today: a bit, yes. If you can follow instructions to
      paste a couple of commands into a terminal, you can do it in an
      evening. Once it's running, no — the app is for everyone in the
      house, and nobody but the person who set it up ever sees a command.
      <%= link_to "What installing involves →", about_page_path("install"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">What if I don't set up a model?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      Then you have a very good private organiser: a calendar across every
      part of your life, a document vault that can find things, tasks,
      people, photos, recipes, a feed reader, a password vault and a diary.
      Anything that needs a model says so plainly instead of breaking.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Is the AI any good, running on my computer?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      Good enough for most of what this app asks it to do, and behind the
      big hosted models at the hardest end. Reading your documents,
      explaining a letter, drafting a formal one, filing things and breaking
      down a goal all work well on a modest model. Long open-ended
      conversation is where you'd notice the difference.
      <%= link_to "The honest version, with hardware →", about_page_path("models"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Can it give me medical or legal advice?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      No, and it is built not to try. What it does is preparation and
      comprehension: explaining what a letter or a lab result actually says,
      listing the questions worth asking, and drafting the formal letter
      you were entitled to send anyway. It will tell you a deadline is
      commonly thirty days and that you should check your own state's rules,
      rather than inventing a confident number. That restraint is
      deliberate, and it is the reason the output is worth trusting.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">What happens to my data if this project stops?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      Nothing. It is a database file and a folder of attachments on hardware
      you own, and the software carries on running on the machine it's
      already on. There is no server of ours to switch off, because there
      isn't one now. That is most of the point.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Can my family see my things?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      Only what you have shared with them, deliberately, and you can see
      that list and undo it at any time. The admin account has no special
      view, and two things — the password vault and the diary — are
      encrypted so that even someone with the raw database gets nothing.
      <%= link_to "How households work →", about_page_path("household"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Can I use it on my phone?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      Yes — it's a website, so it works in a phone browser and can be added
      to a home screen like an app. Reaching it from outside the house is
      the ordinary self-hosting question, and the ordinary answers apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Is it finished?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      No. A great deal works and is in daily use; some of what is described
      across these pages is newer than the rest; and the roadmap is public
      rather than promised. If a page here describes something, it exists —
      we'd rather have fewer pages than aspirational ones.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="display text-xl">Why is it free?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-soft">
      Because charging rent for it would contradict the argument for
      building it. There is no paid tier, no hosted version, and nothing
      held back — the whole thing is the thing you can run.
      <%= link_to "The longer answer →", about_page_path("why"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
